---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Install from '../components/Install.astro';
import HeroJourney from '../components/HeroJourney.astro';
import Examples from '../components/Examples.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import brandLogo from '../assets/logo_dumbdiagrams_highres.png';
import websitePackage from '../../package.json';
import { url, npmUrl, repository } from '../lib/site';
const releaseVersion = websitePackage.version;
const structuredData = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
  name: 'Dumb Diagrams', applicationCategory: 'DeveloperApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Windows, macOS, Linux', runtimePlatform: 'Node.js 24+',
  description: 'LLM-first architecture diagrams as code: readable .dgm source, deterministic layout, a local visual editor, and SVG export.',
  license: 'https://spdx.org/licenses/AGPL-3.0-only.html',
  isAccessibleForFree: true, downloadUrl: npmUrl,
  ...(Astro.site ? { url: new URL(url(), Astro.site).href } : {}),
  ...(repository ? { sameAs: repository } : {}),
};
---
<Base structuredData={structuredData}>
  <main id="main">
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      <div class="hero-eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow"><i></i> LLM-FIRST ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAMS</span><span class="version mono">OPEN SOURCE / v{releaseVersion}</span></div>
      <div class="hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <h1 id="hero-title">A little opinionated.<br /><span>A lot clearer<span class="hero-period">.</span></span></h1>
          <div class="hero-description"><p>Architecture diagrams as code, built for an LLM-first workflow. Draft with a coding agent, refine locally in Studio, and keep your <code>.dgm</code> source in Git.</p></div>
          <div class="hero-start"><Install /></div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-terminal"><HeroJourney /></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="principles-strip" aria-label="Product principles"><span>WHAT YOU GET</span><p>LLM-first</p><b aria-hidden="true">+</b><p>Diagrams as code</p><b aria-hidden="true">+</b><p>Local by default</p><b aria-hidden="true">+</b><p>Open source</p></div>

    <Examples />

    <section class="manifesto section" data-reveal aria-labelledby="manifesto-title">
      <div class="manifesto-main"><span class="eyebrow">02 / LESS GUESSWORK, MORE ENGINE</span><h2 id="manifesto-title">Let the agent describe it.<br /><em>Let the engine work it out.</em></h2><p>Give the model the architecture, not a canvas full of coordinates. The engine calculates placement and connector routes. You get a repeatable first render—and the final say on how it looks.</p><a class="button light" href={url('docs/routing/')}>Inside the engine <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
      <div class="manifesto-notes"><article><span class="mono">01 / DETERMINISTIC ROUTING</span><h3>Same source. Same result.</h3><p>The same document, catalog, and saved view produce the same output. Routes are scored for obstacles, crossings, bends, and distance, with A* search when simple paths are blocked. Dense cases surface warnings—not promises of perfection.</p></article><article><span class="mono">02 / YOUR FINAL SAY</span><h3>Automatic doesn’t mean locked.</h3><p>Move a service, choose a port, or draw a manual route in Studio. Your positions and visual overrides stay in the <code>.dgm</code> file, so the next render preserves your decisions.</p></article><article><span class="mono">03 / LLM-FIRST, TOKEN-CONSCIOUS</span><h3>Spend tokens on the system.</h3><p>Ask an agent to write compact source instead of issuing repeated shape-by-shape MCP tool calls. Offloading layout to the engine can reduce token use and make lighter models practical. Savings depend on the model, context, and workflow.</p></article></div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq section" aria-labelledby="faq-title"><div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">03 / PRACTICAL QUESTIONS</span><h2 id="faq-title">What to expect.</h2><p>A few things to know before you start.</p><div class="faq-brand" aria-hidden="true"><Image src={brandLogo} width={440} widths={[440, 880, 1320]} sizes="(max-width: 800px) 324px, 440px" quality={95} format="webp" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div></div><div class="faq-list">
      <details><summary>What does LLM-first diagrams as code mean?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Your architecture lives in a readable <code>.dgm</code> file, not a collection of hand-placed shapes. A large language model (LLM) can draft or update that source through a coding agent; the deterministic engine handles layout and routing. Review the diff in Git, validate it with the CLI, and refine the result in Studio. No built-in model or AI subscription is required. <a href={url('docs/agent-workflow/')}>Explore the LLM-first workflow.</a></p></details>
      <details><summary>Do I need a coding agent?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>No, but we strongly recommend using one for your first draft. That’s the workflow Dumb Diagrams was built for. Describe your system and give your agent the context generated for your project: <code>DUMB_DIAGRAMS.md</code>, <code>examples/README.md</code>, <code>CATALOG.md</code>, <code>CATALOG.json</code>, and a relevant example. With those in hand, Codex, Claude Code, Cursor, Antigravity, or another coding agent should give you a solid starting point. You can also write <code>.dgm</code> files yourself or edit them in Studio.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Where do my diagrams live?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>On your machine, as <code>.dgm</code> files. In normal CLI use, they never leave your device: Studio runs at <code>127.0.0.1</code> and reads and saves them directly. Your project files are yours, and we don’t upload or store your diagrams on our servers. If you use the standalone browser editor instead, it keeps your work in browser storage, and you download a copy when you want one. <a href={url('docs/studio/')}>More about the two modes.</a></p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I use it for clouds other than AWS?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Yes. AWS was the starting point, and the catalog now includes Azure, Google Cloud, and cloud-agnostic assets too. Support for those may be less polished than the AWS workflow. If something you need for your provider is missing, contributions are welcome. Here’s <a href={url('docs/contributing/')}>how to get involved</a>.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How is this different from a whiteboard?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>You describe the services, boundaries, and connections, and Dumb Diagrams lays them out for you. You can then refine the result. The engine keeps placement, icon alignment, and arrow routing consistent, so an agent’s draft won’t leave you with drifting icons or arrows taking odd paths. An agent may give you a different draft each time, but the same <code>.dgm</code> source always renders the same way. The layout rules mostly follow AWS architecture guidance, with a few design choices of our own. It’s built for architecture diagrams, so it won’t cover general illustration, track your live infrastructure, or give you a hosted workspace for editing together.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Is it free and open source?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Yes. Dumb Diagrams is released under AGPL-3.0-only. The provider icons come with their own terms. You’ll find the details in the <a href={url('LICENSE.txt')}>project license</a> and <a href={url('ICON-NOTICES.txt')}>icon notices</a>.</p></details>
    </div></section>

    <section class="closing" data-reveal><div><span class="eyebrow">START WITH ONE DIAGRAM</span><h2>Put the architecture<br /><em>on the page.</em></h2></div><a href={url('docs/quick-start/')} class="closing-cta trace-border" data-trace>Create your first diagram <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
  </main>
</Base>
